# First open-dialogue experience

User-confirmed product rule (2026-10-01): open AI conversation is a core RPG selling point. Every game that offers it must teach its actual effect early, across text, cinematic and spatial shells.

- After a character's visible introduction, explicitly offer “自由对话 / Ask in your own words” in the prologue or first safe interaction. Do not hide the only entry under a vague “something else” label. Cinematic opening beats still finish before the first input opportunity.
- Create a real loop: player writes an editable, situation-relevant question; manually sends it; receives the live model's contextual response; can follow up or return to the current objective. A suggested question may help, but never auto-send it or substitute canned dialogue for a claimed AI demonstration.
- Use only introduced characters and discovered information. Verify the same onboarding state through the real backend: a UI invitation is broken if an unrelated mainline completion gate rejects it before the model runs.
- Keep dialogue admission separate from mainline action admission. Once the character is introduced and interaction is allowed, do not require completion of the entire prologue merely to chat. Preserve scene/proximity, input, ownership and output validation; enabling chat does not enable travel, rewards or endings. Distinguish a definite story-gate rejection from network/model failure in bilingual recovery messages instead of showing a generic retry loop.
- Explain what dialogue can actually do. In narration-only games, conversation supports understanding but does not independently grant objects, establish evidence or finish tasks. Where actions are supported, teach the authoritative effect, not merely the prose claim.
- Derive completion from durable authoritative successful dialogue, not a clicked button or local success flag. Failure retains the draft and current progress; a scripted route and later retry remain available. AI uptime must not become an unavoidable onboarding lock.
- Verify an early zh/en live exchange, preserved story invariants, failure/reload recovery, and 320×568 / 390×844 layouts. Record capability verification separately from new-player comprehension; without human evidence, use `comprehension unverified`.

Reuse this contract, not a sample game's names, rooms, questions, fixed dialogue count or task flags. For existing games, preserve saves and add this as a presentation/admission correction rather than resetting the campaign.

## Gradual first visit

Introduce identity, immediate need and the first reachable place over player-paced beats rather than one dense briefing. Let the player cause one small visible result before explaining the next system; the current objective should name the place and action, so the player need not remember a paragraph of directions. Opening a menu or revisiting a character must not restart the entire introduction. In a spatial game, retain approach → explicit interaction → reply → follow-up rather than triggering dialogue on proximity alone. A suggested question remains editable and is never sent automatically. Keep a usable authored route when the model is unavailable.

Choose the number and length of beats for the story and test readability with actual locales and viewports. Harbor's short arrival sequence, early question and visible first task provide a worked verification example, not a mandatory three-page prologue or universal quest sequence. A completed scripted walkthrough establishes operability; without a new player's own explanation or behavior, comprehension remains unverified.

## Evidence and transfer boundary · 2026-10-01

Before the Close demonstrated an early real public exchange in both languages, a manually typed question from the explicit coaching button, a contextual reply, a success explanation and return to the authored investigation. Refresh retained history and did not repeat the first-time invitation. The original failure was a main-flow prologue gate applied before the narrator, not quota exhaustion. This is a verified example, not proof of service uptime or new-player comprehension (`comprehension unverified`).

Apply [dialogue-continuity.md](dialogue-continuity.md) to what the player sees before and after the reply. Keep the model-success record, story effects and presentation reading cursor separate.
